          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is it ever correct to submit a code root without its decimal characters?",
          "answer": "It is generally acceptable to submit an ICD-10 code root without its decimal characters when referencing the three-character category codes. For example, the code for essential hypertension is \"I10,\" which is considered complete without a decimal. In contrast, other codes may require additional characters and a decimal point to provide further specificity, such as \"E11\" for type 2 diabetes, which is never used alone in its three-character form.\n\nAccording to the information extracted from the document on \"ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection,\" a code root may stand alone without decimal characters only if it is a complete category code. However, when codes are extended after the initial three characters, a decimal point is necessary to indicate the format (e.g., E11.9) (source: \"ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection\").\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
